raphael (Redmi K20 Pro, SM8150): panel brightness over DSI, ported from HakiOS.

- raphael's panel is an AMOLED (Samsung EA8076) with no backlight; the Poco F1's WLED path (0242/0243) does nothing
  on it. Its brightness is DCS "set display brightness" (0x51), 11 bits, 2..2047 (device tree: bl_ctrl_dcs,
  bl-min-level 2, bl-max-level 0x7ff).
- Source: HakiOS (drivers/gpu/hakios.adreno.c, raphael_dsi_send() and raphael_dsi_brightness()): the same packet,
  registers, values and order, through the DSI0 controller's DMA command engine (0x0ae94000: DMA_CTRL 0x3c,
  DMA_CMD_OFFSET 0x48, DMA_CMD_LENGTH 0x4c, TRIG_CTRL 0x80, DMA_SW_TRIGGER 0x90; DMA_CTRL and TRIG_CTRL restored),
  command buffer at 0x9de00000 in the firmware's display region, and HakiOS's level curve (2 + N^2 * 2045 / 10^6).
- New bsd/dev/sweet_dsibl.c, started from sweet_ufs.c with the other board drivers. Boot-arg sweetbl (off by
  default): 1 logs the DSI0 registers (reads only); 2 serves kern.sweet_brightness (0..1000, writable by any user; a read gives the last
  level sent). Nothing is sent unless the controller reads as enabled; 0 is the dimmest level, never off. One worker
  sends, requests are coalesced, at most 50 commands a second.
- SweetFramebuffer.cpp: the backlight service's setup moves into AppleCLCD::blPublish() and raphael gets the
  service too (it was inside the Poco F1 block; still only with sweetfb_blsvc=1, off by default). A level iOS sets
  on it (0..4095, light) goes to sweet_dsibl_request_light(): level 2 + sqrt(L / 4095) * 2045; a no-op without
  sweetbl=2.

Checked on the phone (2026-11-14, local build, sweetbl=2, iOS 18.7.8 root, over SSH): kern.sweet_brightness 200,
1000, 30 and 500 each changed the panel's brightness (levels 83, 2047, 3, 513); DSI0 reported the DMA command done
(INT_CTRL bit 0) and no ACK error.
With sweetfb_blsvc=1 backboardd found the service (AppleARMBacklight) and then crashed about 10 s after each start,
in a loop: EXC_BAD_ACCESS at address 0 inside CoreBrightness, before it set any level. So iOS's own slider does not
work yet, the service stays off by default, and the light curve of that path has not run on the phone.
iOS's slider works instead through a hook in backboardd (tools/raphael/blhook/sweet_bl_hook.c, as HakiOS does it),
which writes kern.sweet_brightness; backboardd is not root, hence CTLFLAG_ANYBODY. Tried on the phone 2026-11-15.

